A||N||6||polomeno||How hard is it to replace E-Brake cables??||||1||
Z||000000||polomeno||10-06-2000||10:33 AM||||Hey guys.. my e-brake cable needs to be replaced.. when i pull the lever the cable it is connected to moves fine... but the 2 back cables are not moving at all.. i disconnected them from the calipers and they dont move at all.. i went to garage and they said it's around $250 + for parts and labour to do this job.. how difficult would it be to trouble shoot the problem and replace the required cables? I have done some work on my car myself ( replaced back brake calipers, and both clutch cylinders , window regulator ) also if anyone has any tips on doing this job would be appreciated<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000001||polomeno||10-06-2000||10:34 AM||||sorry for double post.. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||LAMBO||10-06-2000||12:01 PM||||I just did this to one of my cars a couple of days ago. Very easy, took all of 1 hour. But I also changed the main cable back from the lever.<BR>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000003||Zorba||10-06-2000||12:52 PM|||| Don't take your fiero to shops. They always mess something up in the process. Unless you know them really well. The fiero does better with a do-it-yourself approach.||||reg||1
Z||000004||polomeno||10-06-2000||01:11 PM||||any tips or things i should know before i attempt this ??||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||10-06-2000||01:13 PM||||I replaced the front and both rear cables with new ones from the Fiero Store. The front one was the most trouble to get out and the new one in. <P>I had the additional problem that the lever on both the left and right hand caliper didn't move freely so I had to put reman'd calipers on both sides in the rear.<P>One more problem was that the front cable was too long. I had to make a spacer to effectively shorten the front cable where it goes through the equalizer in the back of the car.<P>If I hadn't had to put the new calipers on, it wouldn't have taken more than a day. It might be less for anyone with some mechanical skill.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jay||10-06-2000||06:16 PM||||It's pretty easy.  Usually the front line is fine it's the left and right ones that seize up because they are exposed to the eliments. Just by new left and right cables and make sure you don't mix them up.  I suggest buying a Hayes manual, for a couple of bucks it has half decent illustrations and proceedures.  Don't pay $250 bucks for E-cables.  Just remember how everything came off and put it back the same way.||||reg||1
